-----Breakdown-----
Yes. crontab -e opens your personal cron schedule, and you can put multiple jobs in that one file—one job per line
--crontab -e

minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week command
| Position | Allowed values | `*` means |
|---|---:|---|
| Minute | `0–59` | every minute |
| Hour | `0–23` | every hour |
| Day of month | `1–31` | every day |
| Month | `1–12` | every month |
| Day of week | `0–7` (`0` and `7` = Sunday) | every day of the week |

*/5 * * * * command      # every 5 minutes --The /5 means “step by 5.” || So it runs at: :00, :05, :10, :15, :20, :25, :30, :35, :40, :45, :50, :55
0 * * * * command        # at the start of every hour
0 9 * * 1-5 command      # 9 AM, Monday through Friday
0 21 * * * command       # 9 PM every day
0 0 1 * * command         # midnight on the first of each month

*/15 * * * * command     # every 15 minutes
0 */2 * * * command       # every 2 hours, at :00
0 9-17/2 * * * command    # every 2 hours from 9 AM through 5 PM
-----

-------------------Examples

# Write a test message every day at 9 PM
0 21 * * * echo "hello from Cronie - $(/usr/bin/date)" >> /home/sysadmin/Documents/cronie/cronie-hello.log 2>&1

# Run a backup at 2 AM daily
0 2 * * * /home/sysadmin/scripts/backup.sh >> /home/sysadmin/Documents/cronie/backup.log 2>&1

# Clean temporary files every Sunday at 3 AM
0 3 * * 0 /home/sysadmin/scripts/cleanup.sh >> /home/sysadmin/Documents/cronie/cleanup.log 2>&1

-------------Monitoring
--crontab -l
--systemctl status cronie.service --service checks
--journalctl -u cronie.service -f --service logs
--tail -f ~/Documents/cronie/cronie-hello.log

